WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:06.081
Welcome to Part 7, the last part:
older systems and retrofits.

2
00:00:06.381 --> 00:00:10.701
This is the only part where we talk
about the older setups. By the end,

3
00:00:10.701 --> 00:00:17.158
you'll know how to tell them apart, what never to
do on one, and the three ways an old job gets fixed.

4
00:00:18.308 --> 00:00:22.193
On a service call, first find out
which system you're standing under.

5
00:00:22.493 --> 00:00:26.609
There are three. Mark
1, Mark 2, and Mark 3.

6
00:00:26.909 --> 00:00:30.404
Tell them apart by the board,
not by the supply label.

7
00:00:31.554 --> 00:00:35.938
Mark 1 is the old boxes, from
our first jobs. A 350 watt,

8
00:00:35.938 --> 00:00:41.034
12 volt supply, one wire per
injection, and no breakers at all.

9
00:00:41.334 --> 00:00:48.861
Mark 2 is our box with the old board, at 24 volts,
with a supply anywhere from 450 to 600 watts.

10
00:00:48.861 --> 00:00:56.559
It often has one 14/2 feeding several injections,
and only two 5 amp breakers in the whole box.

11
00:00:56.859 --> 00:01:03.562
Mark 3 is the current standard. Six breakers,
4 amps each, one feeder per circuit,

12
00:01:03.562 --> 00:01:11.741
and the positive cut at every injection. It runs
on a 600 watt or a 450 watt supply, at 24 volts.

13
00:01:12.041 --> 00:01:15.776
So a 450 watt label on its
own doesn't make it old.

14
00:01:15.776 --> 00:01:19.761
Count the breakers. Six 4
amp breakers is a Mark 3.

15
00:01:20.911 --> 00:01:24.305
Mark 1 or Mark 2? Don't
change it in the field.

16
00:01:24.605 --> 00:01:28.233
Write down the address, take
photos of the box and the runs,

17
00:01:28.233 --> 00:01:32.233
and tell your crew lead. It gets
booked for a proper retrofit.

18
00:01:32.533 --> 00:01:38.035
Mark 3 is normal service, inside
the limits. On a 450 watt Mark 3,

19
00:01:38.035 --> 00:01:41.952
total every circuit
before you add anything.

20
00:01:43.102 --> 00:01:46.748
Stop, and call your crew
lead, if you see any of these.

21
00:01:47.048 --> 00:01:51.520
Lights wired straight into the
supply, with no breaker or fuse.

22
00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:54.596
One wire feeding two or more injections,

23
00:01:54.596 --> 00:01:57.761
or more than 70 lights between injections.

24
00:01:58.061 --> 00:01:59.579
Any 12 volt system.

25
00:01:59.879 --> 00:02:02.672
A breaker that trips when
the customer picks white.

26
00:02:02.972 --> 00:02:07.939
Wire through a wall with no rating
number on it. Wire that looks brown,

27
00:02:07.939 --> 00:02:11.686
stiff or melted, or smells
burnt. Or solid copper.

28
00:02:12.836 --> 00:02:15.552
And three things you
never do on an old system.

29
00:02:15.852 --> 00:02:18.672
Never add lights to a Mark 1 or a Mark 2,

30
00:02:18.672 --> 00:02:22.017
however few. Those
circuits are already full.

31
00:02:22.317 --> 00:02:24.825
Never fit a bigger breaker to stop a trip.

32
00:02:25.125 --> 00:02:28.562
And never leave an old run
live without metering it,

33
00:02:28.562 --> 00:02:31.257
even if all you did was swap a light.

34
00:02:32.407 --> 00:02:35.471
If the customer asks what's
going on, keep it simple.

35
00:02:35.771 --> 00:02:38.649
We moved to a new power
system. It's more reliable,

36
00:02:38.649 --> 00:02:42.906
and it protects the circuit better.
You're on our list for the upgrade.

37
00:02:43.206 --> 00:02:46.354
No talk of fire, and no
blame. If they keep asking,

38
00:02:46.354 --> 00:02:50.122
it goes to your crew lead, so
they get a straight answer.

39
00:02:51.272 --> 00:02:53.906
There are three ways
an old job gets fixed.

40
00:02:54.206 --> 00:02:59.232
Fix A. New board. For an old job that
already has one wire per injection,

41
00:02:59.232 --> 00:03:01.066
but never had breakers.

42
00:03:01.366 --> 00:03:05.984
Power off. Fit the new board. Each
wire on its own 4 amp circuit,

43
00:03:05.984 --> 00:03:12.772
and the controller on its own. Count the lights
in every group, and split any group over 70.

44
00:03:13.072 --> 00:03:18.249
Fix B. New wire. For an old job where
one wire feeds several injections.

45
00:03:18.249 --> 00:03:21.303
There's no way around pulling new wire.

46
00:03:21.603 --> 00:03:26.246
Pull new 16/2 to every injection
point, one 4 amp circuit per wire,

47
00:03:26.246 --> 00:03:29.268
and cut the positive at every injection.

48
00:03:29.568 --> 00:03:36.684
Fix C. Small driver. A small Class 2 driver right at
the plug, instead of pulling wire all the way back.

49
00:03:36.684 --> 00:03:42.477
Mount it in an enclosure, cut the positive,
and the data still runs from the box.

50
00:03:42.777 --> 00:03:46.759
Every fix ends the same
way. On a 450 watt supply,

51
00:03:46.759 --> 00:03:50.832
check the total. Then
meter every circuit on PW.

52
00:03:51.982 --> 00:03:53.006
Let's lock it in.

53
00:03:53.306 --> 00:03:58.100
Find out which system it is first. Count
the breakers, don't trust the label.

54
00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:04.738
Mark 1 or Mark 2: no changes in the
field. Address, photos, crew lead.

55
00:04:05.038 --> 00:04:07.702
Never add lights, never a bigger breaker,

56
00:04:07.702 --> 00:04:11.330
and never leave an old run
live without metering it.

57
00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:15.791
Three fixes: new board, new
wire, or a small driver.

58
00:04:15.791 --> 00:04:19.162
Every one ends with a meter test on PW.

59
00:04:19.462 --> 00:04:22.525
That's the end of the
course. Take the Part 7 quiz,

60
00:04:22.525 --> 00:04:24.637
and thanks for wiring it right.
